 Boost Converter for Power Factor Correction (PFC)
\f1\b0\fs24 \

\f0\b\fs32 1.Problem:
\f1\b0 \
Design a boost converter for PFC in an industrial power supply. The input is 85-265V AC, and the output should be 400V DC with low harmonic distortion and high power factor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b\fs28 \cf0 Targets
\fs32 :
\f3\i0\b0  Power Factor (PF) > 0.98 and Total Harmonic Distortion (THD) < 5%.
\f1 \cf2 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\b \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 3. Tasks
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li803\fi-178\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Model the boost converter in continuous conduction mode (CCM) and derive the small-signal transfer functions.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Design a cascade control structure with an inner current loop and an outer voltage loop for PFC, using PI controllers.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Explain the advantages of current mode control for PFC, such as inherent current limiting, improved. line regulation, and better disturbance rejection.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Simulate the converter performance under varying input voltage and load conditions, and evaluate    the power factor and total harmonic distortion (THD).\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \

\f0\b 4. Importance:
\f1\b0 \
PFC design is crucial for compliance with global regulatory standards (e.g., IEC 61000-3-2), maximizing power efficiency, and minimizing harmonic pollution on the utility grid.\
\

\f0\b 5. Market Relevance:
\f1\b0 \
The PFC Boost Converter is the standard front-end topology for nearly all modern high-power AC-DC applications, including server power supplies, EV chargers, and industrial automation equipment.\
}